---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt, section } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import appSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam?raw";
import schemaSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/doc_schema.gleam?raw";
import gleamToml from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/gleam.toml?raw";

const devConstants = section(
  appSource,
  "/// These dev constants match",
  "pub fn main(",
);
const start = excerpt(appSource, "pub fn main(");
const connectEffect = excerpt(appSource, "fn init(");
const readiness = section(
  appSource,
  "    GotDocument(doc) -> {",
  "    EnsuredNotes(Ok(notes)) ->",
);
const bootstrap = excerpt(appSource, "fn bootstrap_effect(");
const assemble = excerpt(appSource, "fn assemble(");

const beam = `import gleam/erlang/process.{type Subject}
import watershed/map_kernel
import watershed_beam

// Blocks until the document's full history has replayed locally, then
// hands back the document — fine inside an OTP process, but it would
// freeze everything in a browser tab.
let assert Ok(doc) =
  watershed_beam.connect(
    host: "127.0.0.1",
    port: 4000,
    tenant: "dev-tenant",
    document: "retro-tutorial",
    token: token,
    user_id: "ada",
  )

// Root-map changes arrive as a Subject you select on, not as a callback.
let events: Subject(map_kernel.MapEvent) =
  watershed_beam.subscribe(watershed_beam.root(doc))`;

const layout = `retro_tutorial_lustre/
├── gleam.toml
├── package.json
├── pnpm-workspace.yaml
├── build.mjs
├── index.html
├── src/
│   ├── doc_schema.gleam            ← this sheet
│   ├── note.gleam                  ← step 03
│   ├── board.gleam                 ← step 03
│   ├── board_ops.gleam             ← steps 03 and 04
│   └── retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam ← every sheet
└── test/
    ├── note_codec_test.gleam
    ├── board_test.gleam
    └── convergence_test.gleam      ← step 06`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="connect"
  description="Step two of the watershed build guide: connect the tutorial retro board to a live document, wait for it to finish catching up before you build on it, and set up its two maps with ensure_or_map before the first render."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The races were real; nothing was keeping order yet</h2>
    <p>
      Everything you watched on the last sheet ran inside one browser tab, so
      there was no real race — you controlled the timing yourself. With real
      replicas, separate tabs or separate computers, somebody has to referee
      the order edits land in. That referee is the server, and the thing it
      referees is a <strong>document</strong>: one shared board, addressed by a{" "}
      <code>tenant</code> id and a <code>document</code> id. The tenant id
      groups documents; the document id picks one board in that group. Any
      client that opens the same pair of names joins the same live board.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connecting does two things. First it catches you up: the server replays
      the board’s whole history of edits, so you land on the current state
      instead of an empty one. Then it keeps the line open, streaming every
      later edit — yours and everyone else’s — in the single order the server
      assigns.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={devConstants} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={start} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        <code>document_on_navigate</code> reads <code>?document=</code> from the
        URL, or mints a name and puts it there — so two tabs share a board by
        sharing the link
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Connecting is a declared effect, not a callback</h2>
    <p>
      The browser side of watershed talks in callbacks: <code>on_ready</code>,
      subscription handlers, presence events. If you wired those into Lustre by
      hand, each one would need to fire off a message on its own — and firing a
      message from inside <code>update</code>, while <code>update</code> is
      still assembling the model you’re about to return, quietly overwrites
      that half-built model with a stale one.{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/tree/main/watershed_lustre">
      <code>watershed_lustre</code></a> does that wiring once, correctly, so
      your <code>init</code> just returns the connection as one more effect —
      the same way it returns any other effect in Lustre.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={connectEffect} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong><code>connect_dev</code> is for your laptop.</strong> It mints a
        dev token in the browser from a shared secret, which is exactly what you
        want against <code>just integration-up</code> and exactly what you do not
        want in production. There, issue the token from a backend and call
        <code>watershed_lustre.connect</code> instead. The rest of this sheet is
        identical either way.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The handle and the handshake are two different events</h2>
    <p>
      Notice that <code>connect_dev</code> fires two separate messages.
      <code>got_document</code> arrives the moment you have something to hold
      onto — a reference to the document, before it’s caught up. <code>connected</code>{" "}
      arrives later, once the server has finished replaying history and you’re
      actually in sync. Treat those as the same moment and you get a subtle
      bug on first load: you start building on a document that looks ready but
      hasn’t caught up yet, and wonder why the board flickers or shows nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the tutorial app keeps both messages separate, and only starts
      building the board once whichever one arrives second has shown up. That
      is the whole reason for the double <code>case</code> below.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={readiness} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Seed the channels with <code>ensure_or_map</code></h2>
    <p>
      A brand-new document starts out empty. If two people open a new board at
      the exact same moment, both of their apps try to create the notes map —
      and exactly one of those attempts has to become the real one, with the
      other simply adopting whichever one won.
      <code>ensure_or_map</code> handles that for you: give it a field name,
      and it either creates the map if nothing’s there yet, or hands you back
      the one that already exists. Either way you get a usable map, with no
      retry logic to write yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      From here on, we’ll call a named, shared field inside a document a{" "}
      <strong>channel</strong> — <code>notes</code> and <code>votes</code> are
      both channels. The schema file is the only place their key strings live.
      Two channels, one plain string field, and a <code>BoardDoc</code> marker
      baked into the types — it costs nothing at runtime, but it ties all three
      fields to this one document, so reading a field against the wrong
      document is a compile error, not a surprise while the app is running.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/doc_schema.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={schemaSource} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={bootstrap} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The two modes are the two races from step 01, made concrete.
      <code>RegisterMode</code> stores one string per key — that’s where whole
      notes go in step 03. <code>TallyMode</code> stores one signed number per
      key — that’s where votes go in step 04. The mode is fixed the moment the
      channel is created, so this is where you choose it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Render only when both channels are home</h2>
    <p>
      Each call to <code>ensure_or_map</code> hands its map back separately,
      and the two don’t arrive together. Render as soon as one shows up and
      you’d get a notes list with no vote totals for a moment, so instead the
      app holds both results in a <code>PendingShared</code> pair and waits to
      render anything until both channels are in.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={assemble} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The subscriptions come last, and they matter more than they look. A
      subscription only tells you about changes that happen <em>after</em> you
      subscribe — it never replays what’s already in the map. That’s why{" "}
      <code>assemble</code> takes a snapshot of the current state in the same
      breath that it subscribes. Skip the snapshot, and the board boots up
      empty and stays empty until someone else happens to type.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>One message for both origins.</strong> A local edit and a remote
        edit both dispatch <code>SharedChanged</code>, and both re-read the board
        through one render path. There is no “my write” branch, because the
        moment you write one you start maintaining two versions of the truth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>Deepening: the same thing on the BEAM</h3>
    <p>
      watershed is built BEAM-first — the BEAM is the Erlang virtual machine
      that Gleam also targets on the server — and the same core logic compiles
      to the browser too. The map operations are identical either way; only the
      connecting and subscribing look different, because the two runtimes work
      differently.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={beam} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        watershed_beam · for comparison only, the tutorial stays in the browser
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      On the BEAM, there’s no two-message dance to design around:{" "}
      <code>connect</code> simply pauses your code until the document has
      fully caught up, so by the time you’re holding a handle, it’s already
      synced. That’s the one real difference between the two runtimes — and
      it’s exactly why the browser path above needs the two-message pattern:
      JavaScript can’t pause and wait the way an OTP process (an
      Erlang-style lightweight process) can.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Run it</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the whole project, so you know where each later sheet is putting
      its file. Nothing below <code>src/</code> exists yet except the schema and
      the app shell.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={layout} lang="text" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      watershed is not published to Hex yet, so the two <code>path</code>
      dependencies resolve because this example lives in <code>examples/</code>
      inside a watershed checkout. In your own project take both as git
      dependencies pinned to a commit; the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed#install">README</a> gives
      the exact lines.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">gleam.toml</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={gleamToml} lang="toml" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Start a floodgate development server on port <code>4000</code> with{" "}
      <code>just integration-up</code>, then:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code
        code={`cd examples/retro_tutorial_lustre
pnpm install
pnpm build
pnpm serve      # http://localhost:8080`}
        lang="sh"
        theme={watershedCodeTheme}
      />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code> and read the status line:
      <code>connected · board ready · 0 notes</code>. The document is live and
      both channels are attached — there is just nothing in them yet. Step 03
      fixes that.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
